"""Instruction-to-specification boundary for deterministic diagram construction."""
SPEC_FIRST_PROMPT_VERSION = 'spec-first:v5-plane-relations'
SPEC_FIRST_INSTRUCTION = '''Translate the instruction into GeometrySpec. Your role ends at mathematical interpretation: identify the named objects, given and defining relations, branch conditions, and requested visible content. Do not supply coordinates, layout parameters, camera, line styles, TikZ, or a verdict about an image. A deterministic constraint compiler chooses those. Preserve the distinction between premises/definitions and conclusions to be proved. The schema is a bounded geometric language; do not disguise unsupported facts as unrelated supported relations.

Declare every named point once. Fresh helper points can specify the direction/support of a named line or ray; set visible=false for them. Use names such as A, A1, Bprime or B'; the visible name must match the instruction (B' rather than Bprime). Keep source_quote as an exact excerpt supporting each relation and solid. Quotes record provenance; they do not replace the mathematical fields.

Relation vocabulary and point order:
- midpoint [P,A,B]; interpolation [P,A,B] with values=[t] for P=A+t(B-A). Use only a fraction specified or defined by the instruction, not a convenient layout choice.
- projection [H,P,A,B]: H is the perpendicular foot of P on supporting line AB. Declare a ray drawing when that support is a ray; the compiler checks the positive-foot branch.
- reflection [Q,P,A,B]: Q is the reflection of P across supporting line AB.
- intersection [P,A,B,C,D]: P=AB intersect CD. Add on_segment/on_ray relations when an intersection branch is specified.
- on_line/on_segment/on_ray [P,A,B]; same_side [P,Q,A,B] strictly on one side of line AB; inside_angle [P,O,X,Y] strictly inside the convex angle XOY.
- equal_length [A,B,C,D,...] is an equality class of consecutive segment endpoint pairs. Express the given equality directly; the compiler derives supported equality loci. In particular it can combine two projection definitions and equal distances to their feet.
- equal_angle [A,V,B,C,W,D]; perpendicular/parallel [A,B,C,D]; distinct [A,B]; noncollinear [A,B,C]. These assert mathematical facts, not display posture.
- length [A,B] with values=[positive length] and unit="mm", "cm", "m" or "" for consistently unitless quantities. Page scale is independent of mathematical units. length_ratio [A,B,C,D] with values=[AB/CD]; shorter [A,B,C,D] means AB < CD. Never replace metric values with only a consequence such as perpendicularity.
- Symbolic lengths use length with expression="a", "a√2" or "a/(2√3)" and values=[]. Constant arithmetic, parentheses, square roots, and one shared positive length parameter are supported in 2D and 3D. Preserve the exact expression; do not set a=1 or supply a decimal approximation. Expand chains into one length relation per segment. Different independent parameters or mixtures of parameter lengths and numeric lengths are unsupported.
- angle [A,V,B] with values=[degrees] fixes the given non-reflex angle AVB strictly between 0 and 180 degrees, in 2D or 3D. The presentation policy can display its supplied value. Do not compute an unknown angle and insert its answer as a premise. For a requested unmeasured sector, use draw kind=angle with [A,V,B] and an optional unknown-angle letter instead. Angle sectors always bind the specified rays; they do not by themselves define a line-plane or dihedral angle.
- centroid [G,A,B,C]; parallelogram [A,B,C,D] in cyclic order.
- on_plane [P,A,B,C] places P in the nondegenerate plane ABC. perpendicular_planes [A,B,C,D,E,F] means plane ABC is perpendicular to plane DEF. plane_angle with those six points and values=[degrees] constrains the unoriented angle between the full planes, strictly greater than 0 and at most 90. Do not confuse this with a line-plane relation. The compiler computes the actual intersection and a perpendicular cross-section.
- dihedral_angle [A,B,C,D] with values=[degrees] fixes the angle of the two HALF-planes containing A and D bounded by hinge BC: notation [A,BC,D]. It may be obtuse, strictly between 0 and 180. A and D must lie off BC. Do not replace it by the acute angle between the supporting planes or by angle ABD. For a requested unknown dihedral use draw kind=dihedral_angle with these four points and optionally an unknown-angle letter; for an unknown angle between full planes use draw kind=plane_angle with two triples. No numerical answer is inferred. The deterministic renderer supplies true normal-section carriers, so no arbitrary helper coordinates or special-case auxiliary angles are needed.
- partition [A,P1,...,Pn,B] in order along a segment, with values equal to the positive successive interval ratios, or [] when all intervals are equal.
- parallel_section [S,A,B,...,P,Q,...]: a plane parallel to the base polygon A,B,... cuts the rays SA,SB,... at P,Q,... in corresponding order. The first section point P must be defined on SA by another relation. The compiler derives the other intersections from P and the base directions; do not independently assume their fractions.

Use a solid declaration for a tetrahedron, pyramid or parallelepiped, with the vertex order stated in its field. The boundary edges are automatic. A parallelepiped implies its corresponding translations and parallelogram faces. A pyramid does not imply a parallelogram base: include that relation when given. Derived facts that the instruction expressly withholds must not be substituted for defining intersections.

Drawing scope uses segment, polyline, polygon, line, ray, circle, angle, plane_angle and dihedral_angle. For circle use [center,through]. For angle use [A,V,B]; the existing drawn rays VA and VB must carry that sector. Plane and dihedral drawings use the point orders above and supply their own normal-section carriers. Infinite lines/rays use two support points and an optional object label d/x/y; code chooses finite visible extents and emits ray arrows. Do not label/draw arbitrary helper endpoints. Add requested interior or section edges explicitly. Solid edges do not need duplicate draw entries. Draw only the requested geometry and the carriers needed to show the named construction.

Requested length annotations belong in length_labels, with the endpoint pair, numeric value and unit OR expression and value=null, plus the exact source quote. The matching length relation and drawn carrier are required. Put every unsupported instruction excerpt in unsupported; any nonempty list stops drawing. A sphere is not a circle, and plane projection is not line projection. The current spatial backend implements straight convex solids with one common metric scale; it does not implement curved surfaces.

Relation marks are separate from truth. Use mark=true for an explicit source annotation request, mark=false for an explicit source suppression, and otherwise leave mark=null. The deterministic policy selects cues from supplied perpendicular, numeric angle, equality and equal-partition relations on drawn carriers; repeated given measures form equality classes. It normally communicates parallelism structurally, and can honor requested matching parallel arrows. Requested length labels remain individually required. Do not add derived premises solely to obtain marks. All planar carriers are solid in this bounded method. For a spatial closed convex solid, the renderer computes occlusion from the realized faces and common view; being auxiliary is not a reason for dashed lines. If a statement requests a different unsupported style/convention, extraction cannot claim that this interface expresses it.

Return only the structured specification. There is no rendered-image review or TikZ repair turn in this method.'''


def spec_first_messages(instruction: str) -> list[dict]:
    return [{'role':'system','content':SPEC_FIRST_INSTRUCTION}, {'role':'user','content':instruction}]
